/* LBO module: lessons, cards and lesson quizzes. Questions follow the Red Book (1st ed.) order; answers are IBZY study notes. */
window.IBZY_CONTENT["lbo"] = {"units": {"lbo-u1": [{"id": "lbo-u1-l1", "title": "How LBOs work & what makes a good target", "pages": "91–95", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u1-1", "q": "What is a leveraged buyout (LBO)?", "a": "<p>In an LBO, a private equity firm (the <b>financial sponsor</b>) buys a company and funds most of the price with debt — loans, bonds and other instruments. The sponsor lines up the financing before closing and contributes the rest as equity.</p><p>With majority control, it then works to make the business generate more cash — operational improvements, restructuring, asset sales — so the heavy debt load can be paid down quickly.</p><p>Sponsors typically hold for <b>5–7 years</b>, then exit by selling to another PE firm or a strategic buyer, or by taking the company public. They usually target returns of roughly <b>20–25%</b>.</p><p><i>Interview note:</i> in PE recruiting, getting the interview is the hardest part, and interviewers often assume you know the technicals. First rounds lean towards discussions of your investing interests, deal experience and thought process — e.g. a portfolio company you would or wouldn’t have backed, a sector or theme you like, or how you’d diligence a new opportunity. They sound behavioural but quickly reveal whether you understand the fundamentals. Bain’s annual Global Private Equity Report is good background reading.</p>", "p": 91}, {"id": "lbo-u1-2", "q": "Explain the basic concept of an LBO to me using a real-life example.", "a": "<p>Think of <b>flipping a house with mostly borrowed money</b>.</p><p>You find a house selling cheaply and think you can sell it later for more. You put down a small deposit and a mortgage lender funds the rest — and you owe the full loan plus interest.</p><p>You don’t live in it; it’s an investment you plan to sell in about five years. You rent out the rooms, and the rental income pays the interest, chips away at the mortgage principal and funds renovations and repairs.</p><p>Five years later you sell for more than you paid — thanks to the improvements and a neighbourhood where prices have risen. You repay what’s left of the mortgage, and because you’ve been paying down the principal all along, you keep a bigger share of the sale proceeds. That’s an LBO: the rent is the company’s cash flow, the renovations are operational improvements, and the deposit is the sponsor’s equity.</p>", "p": 92}, {"id": "lbo-u1-3", "q": "What is the intuition underlying the usage of debt in an LBO?", "a": "<p>LBOs are funded mostly with debt and a relatively small equity cheque. As the company’s cash flow pays down that debt over the holding period, more of the company’s value belongs to the equity — so returns at exit are magnified.</p><p>Debt helps because it’s <b>cheaper</b> than equity: it ranks higher in the capital structure and interest is tax-deductible (the tax shield). More leverage makes it easier to hit the target return — so sponsors push leverage as high as they can while keeping it manageable enough to avoid bankruptcy risk.</p>", "p": 92}, {"id": "lbo-u1-4", "q": "What is the typical capital structure prevalent in LBO transactions?", "a": "<p>It shifts with the financing environment, but the long-run trend has gone from roughly <b>80/20</b> debt/equity in the 1980s to around <b>60/40</b> more recently.</p><p><b>Debt:</b> the bulk is senior secured bank and institutional loans — a revolving credit facility and term loans. Riskier layers sit on top: senior and subordinated notes, high-yield bonds and mezzanine capital.</p><p><b>Equity</b> comes mostly from the sponsor. Management sometimes rolls over part of its equity to share in the upside, and since most LBOs keep the existing team, sponsors typically set aside about <b>3–20%</b> of the equity as a management incentive pool tied to hitting targets.</p>", "p": 92}, {"id": "lbo-u1-5", "q": "What are the main levers in an LBO that drive returns?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Debt paydown (deleveraging):</b> the company’s free cash flow repays debt, so the equity’s share of the business grows over time.</li><li><b>EBITDA growth:</b> operational improvements to margins (cost cuts, price increases), new growth strategies and accretive add-on acquisitions.</li><li><b>Multiple expansion:</b> ideally exiting at a higher multiple than entry — helped by better sentiment, stronger economic conditions, more scale or diversification, and favourable deal dynamics (e.g. a competitive auction with strategic bidders).</li></ul>", "p": 92}, {"id": "lbo-u1-6", "q": "What attributes make a business an ideal LBO candidate?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Strong, predictable free cash flow</b> with high margins — essential to service and repay heavy debt year after year, and visible in the historical numbers.</li><li><b>Recurring revenue</b> — long-term contracts, or products/services customers <i>need</i> to keep operating rather than discretionary spend — means lower-risk cash flows.</li><li><b>An economic moat</b> — a sustainable competitive advantage that protects share and profits: brand, patents, proprietary technology, scale, network effects, switching costs.</li><li><b>Good unit economics</b> — high margins, a well-managed cost structure, low capex and low working capital needs, leaving more cash for interest, debt paydown and reinvestment. Consistently better unit economics than peers often signals a competitive advantage.</li><li><b>A strong, committed management team</b> with a track record — they execute the plan.</li><li><b>A low purchase multiple</b> — harder to find as more capital floods private markets, but opportunistic deals happen when a sector is temporarily out of favour. A cheaper entry increases the chance of multiple expansion and reduces the risk of overpaying.</li><li><b>Value-add opportunities</b> — a well-run business that still has inefficiencies to fix: non-core assets to sell, costs to cut, better sales and marketing.</li></ul>", "p": 93}, {"id": "lbo-u1-7", "q": "What types of industries attract more deal flow from financial buyers?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Non-cyclical, low-growth, mature industries:</b> once organic growth slows, companies turn to acquisitions to keep growing — which means strategic buyers exist for an eventual exit. Low disruption risk and fragmented markets also suit “buy-and-build” add-on strategies.</li><li><b>Subscription / contract-based models:</b> recurring, stable revenue from reliable commercial customers — why B2B enterprise software is so sought after and so richly valued.</li><li><b>High R&amp;D requirements:</b> incumbents would rather buy proven technology than build it, so PE firms back niche players knowing strategics will want them later. These companies often have pricing power and an established niche.</li><li><b>Synergy potential:</b> e.g. industrial tech and software for upselling and cross-selling; healthcare services like dental clinics or psychiatric treatment centres, whose inefficient cost structures benefit from scale and streamlining.</li><li><b>Favourable trends:</b> industries with steady, incremental improvements (and potential add-ons) rather than disruptive shifts that would require heavy reinvestment.</li></ul>", "p": 93}, {"id": "lbo-u1-8", "q": "What would be the ideal type of products/services of a potential LBO target?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Mission-critical:</b> so embedded in customers’ operations that they can’t function without it.</li><li><b>Recurring / contract-based:</b> long-term agreements, e.g. enterprise software subscriptions with commercial customers.</li><li><b>High switching costs:</b> moving to a competitor costs more than any saving from a cheaper provider.</li><li><b>Highly technical:</b> higher barriers to entry and more pricing power, so steadier cash flows with the option to raise prices. Proxies: high R&amp;D spend, patents/IP, industry reputation. Revenue quality comes down to predictability, defensibility and how reliably it recurs.</li><li><b>Location-based competition:</b> some firms target local service businesses (e.g. landscaping, commercial cleaning) — fragmented markets with less competition and often long-term contracts.</li></ul>", "p": 94}, {"id": "lbo-u1-9", "q": "What is the relationship between debt and purchase price?", "a": "<p>Debt lets a sponsor buy a much bigger company than its equity alone could afford. Using lots of debt also leaves the firm more uncommitted capital (“<b>dry powder</b>”) for other deals or add-on acquisitions for existing portfolio companies.</p>", "p": 95}]}, {"id": "lbo-u1-l2", "title": "Leverage, debt capacity & exits", "pages": "95–97", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u1-10", "q": "How is the maximum leverage used in an LBO typically determined?", "a": "<p>Since the deal market stabilised after 2008, PE capital structures have hovered around <b>60% debt / 40% equity</b>, but leverage varies a lot by industry and by the target’s fundamentals.</p><p>Total debt has typically been about <b>5.0–7.0x EBITDA</b>, with upward pressure as valuations rise. In the 1980s, debt was as much as 90% of the capital structure — it has come down because of the risks heavy debt loads create.</p>", "p": 95}, {"id": "lbo-u1-11", "q": "Why might a private equity firm not raise leverage to the maximum leverage, even if it had the option to do so?", "a": "<p>Sponsors want as much debt as the company can safely carry, since less equity means higher returns. But they may deliberately borrow less than lenders would offer:</p><ul><li><b>Default risk:</b> doubts about whether the company can support more debt — even in a stable industry with healthy ratios, it’s a judgement call.</li><li><b>Reputation:</b> PE firms now pitch themselves as value-add partners and don’t want to look like they’re loading companies with debt for their own benefit.</li><li><b>Fund returns and fundraising:</b> a bankrupt portfolio company hurts the fund’s returns, makes future fundraising harder, and makes lenders and target companies warier of working with the firm.</li><li><b>A planned dividend recap:</b> leaving spare debt capacity to borrow later — especially if rates are expected to fall — on better terms.</li></ul>", "p": 95}, {"id": "lbo-u1-12", "q": "What determines a company's debt capacity?", "a": "<p>Usually a bank’s leveraged finance and capital markets teams advise the sponsor. The assessment narrows from the industry down to the company:</p><ol><li><b>Industry risk:</b> growth rate, past cyclicality, barriers to entry, risk of technological disruption, regulation.</li><li><b>Competitive position:</b> how the company compares with the market and whether it has differentiators protecting its revenue and margins, plus substitution risk.</li><li><b>Historical performance and forecast:</b> a model focused on <b>downside</b> scenarios, looking for steady, predictable cash flow — which supports more debt.</li></ol><p>The result is a maximum Total Debt/EBITDA multiple with enough cushion that the company can still meet all its obligations if it underperforms (e.g. EBITDA falls 20–25%). Acceptable leverage and coverage levels vary a lot by industry and lending environment.</p>", "p": 96}, {"id": "lbo-u1-13", "q": "In the context of an LBO, what is the “tax shield”?", "a": "<p>The reduction in taxable income that comes from the heavy debt load: interest is tax-deductible, so more interest means lower taxes. These tax savings are another reason sponsors try to maximise leverage.</p>", "p": 96}, {"id": "lbo-u1-14", "q": "Since senior debt is cheaper, why don't financial sponsors fund the entire debt portion of the capital structure with senior debt?", "a": "<p>Senior lenders will only lend up to a point — usually around <b>2.0–3.0x EBITDA</b> — because default risk rises with every extra dollar of debt. Senior debt is the lowest risk because it ranks first and is secured, but it also comes with the tightest covenants.</p><p>Beyond that level, only more expensive, junior (subordinated) debt is available — less restrictive, but with higher interest rates.</p>", "p": 96}, {"id": "lbo-u1-15", "q": "How do financial sponsors exit their investments?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Sale to a strategic buyer:</b> usually the quickest route and often the highest price, since strategics pay a premium for synergies.</li><li><b>Secondary buyout:</b> sale to another financial sponsor. Financial buyers generally can’t pay for synergies (unless it’s an add-on to an existing portfolio company).</li><li><b>IPO:</b> selling shares to the public. Realistically an option mainly for large companies — mega-funds or club deals.</li></ul>", "p": 96}, {"id": "lbo-u1-16", "q": "What is the one caveat of an IPO exit?", "a": "<p>An IPO isn’t an immediate exit — it’s a path to one. The process is long, and existing shareholders face a <b>lock-up period</b> (typically 90–180 days) during which they can’t sell. The sponsor then sells down its stake over time.</p>", "p": 96}, {"id": "lbo-u1-17", "q": "What is a secondary buyout?", "a": "<p>A sponsor-to-sponsor deal: one PE firm exits by selling the company to another. Evidence suggests secondary buyouts earn <b>lower returns</b> than first-time buyouts, because the previous owner has already captured many of the operational improvements and value-add opportunities.</p>", "p": 97}, {"id": "lbo-u1-18", "q": "What is a dividend recapitalization?", "a": "<p>After an LBO, the sponsor has the company raise <b>new debt</b> and uses the proceeds to pay itself a dividend. It usually happens once some of the original debt has been repaid, freeing up debt capacity.</p><p>It lets the sponsor take some money off the table and de-risk its investment without a full exit (sale or IPO). Getting cash back earlier also boosts the fund’s IRR.</p>", "p": 97}]}, {"id": "lbo-u1-l3", "title": "Value creation, risks & diligence", "pages": "97–98", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u1-19", "q": "How might operating a highly levered company differ from operating a company with minimal or no debt?", "a": "<p>There’s far less margin for error, because large fixed interest and principal payments come due regardless. Leverage makes careful planning and strong financial controls more important, and forces management to be disciplined on costs and conservative with capital spending — especially on new initiatives like expansion or acquisitions.</p>", "p": 97}, {"id": "lbo-u1-20", "q": "How can a private equity firm increase the probability of achieving multiple expansion during the sale process?", "a": "<p>By building a <b>higher-quality business</b> — entering new markets through geographic expansion, developing new products or making strategic add-ons. A bigger, more diversified, better-positioned company tends to command a higher exit multiple.</p><p>Exit multiples can also rise for reasons outside the firm’s control: better market conditions, improved sentiment towards the sector, and favourable deal dynamics such as selling to a strategic.</p>", "p": 97}, {"id": "lbo-u1-21", "q": "Why is multiple expansion viewed as a less than ideal lever for value creation?", "a": "<p>The standard, conservative modelling convention is to assume the exit multiple <b>equals</b> the entry multiple. Future deal conditions are unpredictable, so relying on a higher exit multiple to hit your return target is risky — and if the deal only works with multiple expansion, it may not be a good LBO candidate.</p><p>EBITDA growth and debt paydown are things the sponsor can actually plan for and control; the exit multiple depends on many factors outside its control.</p>", "p": 97}, {"id": "lbo-u1-22", "q": "Can you name a scenario when multiple contraction is common?", "a": "<p>In <b>large</b> LBOs, a small contraction is normal. As a company gets bigger, fewer buyers can afford it, so there’s less competition and a lower price. An IPO is an alternative, depending on the situation.</p><p>A slight contraction doesn’t necessarily hurt returns much — the company’s growth in size implies it also grew revenue and EBITDA and paid down debt.</p>", "p": 97}, {"id": "lbo-u1-23", "q": "What are some risks you would look out for when assessing potential investment opportunities?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Cyclicality:</b> buyout investors prize stable, predictable revenue above everything, and cyclical demand works against that.</li><li><b>Customer concentration:</b> as a rule, no single customer should be more than about 5–10% of revenue.</li><li><b>Customer and employee churn:</b> high customer churn means constantly replacing customers; high staff turnover can point to organisational problems.</li><li><b>Temporarily inflated valuations:</b> buying at the top makes exiting at a higher multiple much harder — e.g. healthcare IT in 2020, when strategics rushed to build digital capabilities during the pandemic.</li><li><b>Past institutional ownership:</b> since secondary buyouts tend to return less, ask what you can do that previous investors couldn’t. Many firms avoid companies previously owned by PE or VC.</li><li><b>Retiring key people:</b> if the business and its customer relationships depend on one person (e.g. the founder) who plans to retire during the hold, the eventual sale gets harder — the next buyer inherits that risk.</li></ul>", "p": 98}, {"id": "lbo-u1-24", "q": "If you had to pick, would you rather invest in a company that sells B2C or B2B?", "a": "<p>All else equal, <b>B2B</b> — its revenue is higher quality. Business customers are more likely to sign long-term contracts, while consumers usually pay month to month.</p><p>Businesses also have far more spending power, are more reliable customers and tend to be more loyal to providers they partner with. The main reason for that “sticky” revenue is high switching costs, and businesses are also less sensitive to price changes.</p>", "p": 98}, {"id": "lbo-u1-25", "q": "Imagine that you're performing diligence on the CIM of a potential LBO investment. Which questions would you attempt to answer?", "a": "<ul><li>Is there a strong management team, and will they stay on?</li><li>What value do the products/services give customers?</li><li>What makes the revenue recurring? Are there long-term contracts?</li><li>Where does management see growth or operational improvement opportunities?</li><li>What has driven recent revenue growth — price, volume, upselling?</li><li>How serious is the competition? Is there a defensible moat?</li><li>What specific value-creation levers can the sponsor pull?</li><li>Is the industry cyclical?</li><li>How concentrated are revenue and end markets?</li><li>Is there a realistic exit, and will there be enough buyer interest when the time comes?</li></ul>", "p": 98}]}, {"id": "lbo-u1-l4", "title": "Deal structuring, add-ons & quick IRR maths", "pages": "99–101", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u1-26", "q": "What is a management buyout (MBO)?", "a": "<p>An LBO where a large share of the post-deal equity comes from the company’s <b>existing management</b>. The premise is that management believes it can run the business better and create more value under its own direction — MBOs often follow a period of underperformance and shareholder pressure.</p><p>Management typically puts in cash and rolls over its existing equity; sponsors or other investors may also contribute equity. The debt side, apart from the rollover, works like a normal LBO.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-27", "q": "What is rollover equity and why do private equity firms perceive it as a positive sign?", "a": "<p>Rollover equity is when existing management reinvests some or all of its equity into the newly acquired company — sometimes adding fresh capital alongside the sponsor. It’s an extra source of funds, reducing the debt and sponsor equity needed.</p><p>It’s a good sign because management is betting its own money that the upside is worth the risk. Having “skin in the game” aligns management’s incentives with the sponsor’s, which benefits everyone.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-28", "q": "When might a PE firm prefer to use term loans rather than subordinated notes in an LBO?", "a": "<p>Term loans are cheaper (lower interest rates), so sponsors usually max out what banks and institutional lenders will provide before turning to riskier financing. They also typically allow optional prepayment, giving more flexibility.</p><p>And if the company plans to be active in M&amp;A — add-ons or divestitures — the restrictive <b>incurrence covenants</b> that come with subordinated notes are a real consideration.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-29", "q": "Would a private equity firm prefer high growth or stability in revenue?", "a": "<p><b>Stability.</b> Because LBOs carry so much debt, traditional buyout firms would almost always choose consistent, predictable revenue over rapid growth if they had to pick one.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-30", "q": "Why might a higher average selling price (ASP) or average order value (AOV) not always be better?", "a": "<p>Pricing power drives revenue, but if the product is too expensive it shrinks the number of customers who can afford it. There has to be a balance between premium pricing (backed by quality) and a market big enough to keep expanding and winning new customers.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-31", "q": "Can a highly capital-intensive industry be appealing to PE investors?", "a": "<p>Asset-light businesses are often preferred because they need less capital to grow. But capital intensity has upsides: it creates <b>high barriers to entry</b>, which deters new competitors, adds stability and gives incumbents collective pricing power.</p><p>Lots of PP&amp;E also helps when raising debt — more fixed assets can be pledged as collateral, increasing the borrowing base and improving lending terms.</p>", "p": 99}, {"id": "lbo-u1-32", "q": "When might customer concentration be considered being at a manageable level?", "a": "<p>When the big customers are locked into <b>irrevocable, long-term contracts</b>. The contractual commitment makes the concentration risk more tolerable — though the buyer may still demand a discount on the price.</p>", "p": 100}, {"id": "lbo-u1-33", "q": "Explain the strategic rationale behind add-on acquisitions and how it creates value.", "a": "<p>An add-on (bolt-on) is when a PE-owned <b>platform</b> company buys a smaller business. The add-on complements the platform’s products or services, creating synergies and extending its reach into new end markets.</p><p>Growth through add-ons is often a core part of the original investment case, and some firms specialise in it: buy a platform in a fragmented market, then consolidate around it (a roll-up). A side benefit is that the platform can compete with strategic buyers in auctions, because it can realise synergies too.</p>", "p": 100}, {"id": "lbo-u1-34", "q": "How can value be created during a consolidation play?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Pricing power:</b> customers pay more for a stronger brand and bundled, complementary offerings.</li><li><b>Bargaining power:</b> a bigger company gets better supplier terms — longer payables (improving the cash conversion cycle) and bulk discounts.</li><li><b>Lower customer acquisition costs:</b> scale, better systems (CRM, ERP) and integrated infrastructure make acquiring customers cheaper.</li><li><b>Better cost structure:</b> economies of scale — combining offices or divisions, removing duplicate functions, cutting overhead (marketing, accounting, IT).</li></ul>", "p": 100}, {"id": "lbo-u1-35", "q": "What does “multiple arbitrage” in a roll-up acquisition scenario imply?", "a": "<p>Smaller add-ons usually trade at <b>lower multiples</b> than the platform. Once acquired, their cash flows are effectively valued at the platform’s higher multiple — creating value immediately, before any operational improvements or integration. That makes add-ons accretive from day one.</p>", "p": 100}, {"id": "lbo-u1-36", "q": "A private equity firm has tripled its initial investment in five years, estimate the IRR?", "a": "<p><b>About 25%</b> (24.6% exactly). You won’t get a calculator, so memorise the common approximations:</p><table><tr><th>Multiple</th><th>3 years</th><th>5 years</th></tr><tr><td>2.0x</td><td>~25%</td><td>~15%</td></tr><tr><td>2.5x</td><td>~35%</td><td>~20%</td></tr><tr><td>3.0x</td><td>~45%</td><td>~25%</td></tr></table>", "p": 100}, {"id": "lbo-u1-37", "q": "How many years would it take to double a $100,000 investment at a 9% annual return?", "a": "<p>About <b>8 years</b>, using the <b>Rule of 72</b>: years to double ≈ 72 ÷ annual return = 72 ÷ 9.</p><p>The lesser-known <b>Rule of 115</b> estimates the time to <i>triple</i>: 115 ÷ annual return.</p>", "p": 101}, {"id": "lbo-u1-38", "q": "If an LBO target had no existing debt on its closing balance sheet, would this increase the returns to the financial buyer?", "a": "<p>Not really. An LBO wipes out the existing capital structure and replaces it with the new financing, so pre-deal debt doesn’t directly affect returns.</p><p>Sponsor returns depend on (1) the initial equity cheque and (2) the cash it receives during the hold — dividends, monitoring fees and above all the exit proceeds.</p><p>A minor point: a management team with no experience running a levered company may struggle with the smaller margin for error, which lenders might flag as a risk — but it’s a small consideration.</p>", "p": 101}]}, {"id": "lbo-u1-l5", "title": "PE funds, fees & how LPs invest", "pages": "101–103", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u1-39", "q": "Where do financial sponsors typically get their capital?", "a": "<p>From <b>limited partners (LPs)</b>: insurance companies, pension funds, sovereign wealth funds, endowments, high-net-worth individuals and financial institutions.</p>", "p": 101}, {"id": "lbo-u1-40", "q": "In the private markets, what does \"dry powder\" mean?", "a": "<p>Capital that LPs have <b>committed</b> to a fund but that hasn’t yet been invested — cash waiting on the sidelines. Record levels of dry powder suggest there are fewer deals meeting PE firms’ criteria; competition for the deals that exist rises, and purchase multiples tend to go up.</p>", "p": 101}, {"id": "lbo-u1-41", "q": "What is proprietary deal sourcing and how does it compare to intermediated deals?", "a": "<p><b>Proprietary deals</b> come from the firm’s own cold outreach or existing relationships. Negotiations tend to be friendlier. Firms often reach out knowing the company isn’t selling yet, hoping to be the first call when it does. The downside: it’s a grind — hundreds of cold calls and emails — and the companies that fit PE criteria are usually successful businesses whose owners are reluctant to sell or give up control.</p><p><b>Intermediated deals</b> are auctions run by an investment bank with a long list of buyers. More competition generally means a higher price, since the sell-side bank is trying to extract the maximum.</p>", "p": 101}, {"id": "lbo-u1-42", "q": "From a limited partner's perspective, what are the advantages/disadvantages of the private equity asset class?", "a": "<p><b>Advantages:</b> target returns of roughly 20–25%+, well above the ~10% average for public equities. PE managers are also active owners, working closely with portfolio companies to create value and cut costs.</p><p><b>Disadvantages:</b> leverage means PE-backed companies carry more bankruptcy risk — the high return is compensation for that. And it’s <b>illiquid</b>: unlike public stock, LPs can’t freely sell their stake.</p>", "p": 101}, {"id": "lbo-u1-43", "q": "Explain the “2 and 20” compensation structure in private equity.", "a": "<p>The standard PE fee structure:</p><ul><li><b>2% management fee</b> on assets under management each year — covers the firm’s running costs (salaries, admin).</li><li><b>20% carried interest</b> — the general partners’ share of the fund’s profits above a pre-agreed hurdle. This is the performance-based incentive.</li></ul>", "p": 102}, {"id": "lbo-u1-44", "q": "What is a distribution waterfall schedule in private equity?", "a": "<p>The order in which investment proceeds are paid out to the different parties, based on the priority of their claims — set by the agreement between the GPs and LPs. A classic waterfall:</p><ol><li>LPs get their initial investment back in full, plus the fund’s minimum <b>hurdle rate</b> (preferred return).</li><li><b>Catch-up:</b> the GP receives profits until it has its 20% share.</li><li>Everything above that is split <b>80% LP / 20% GP</b>.</li></ol><p>The percentages vary, but 80/20 is the industry standard.</p>", "p": 102}, {"id": "lbo-u1-45", "q": "In the distribution waterfall in private equity, what is the catch-up clause?", "a": "<p>Written into the LP agreement: once LPs have received their specified return (usually their capital plus the hurdle rate), the GPs receive most or all of the next profits until their share “catches up” to the agreed split (e.g. 20%). It compensates the GPs for the fact that LPs were paid first.</p>", "p": 102}, {"id": "lbo-u1-46", "q": "What is a clawback provision?", "a": "<p>It lets LPs reclaim carried interest the GPs were <b>overpaid</b>. At the end of the fund, if LPs haven’t received their capital back plus their agreed share of profits, the GPs must return the excess.</p><p>Example: a fund’s first few exits do well and the GPs collect carry, but later investments perform poorly. The clawback lets LPs recover some of the carry that turned out to be overpaid.</p>", "p": 102}, {"id": "lbo-u1-47", "q": "What is the difference between a recapitalization and an LBO?", "a": "<p>An <b>LBO</b> is accounted for as an acquisition — assets are written up and goodwill is recognised.</p><p>A <b>recapitalisation</b> works similarly mechanically but isn’t treated as an acquisition: the asset base carries over unchanged and no goodwill is recognised. Because the price paid is usually above the book value of equity, the result is often <b>negative equity</b> on the balance sheet.</p>", "p": 102}, {"id": "lbo-u1-48", "q": "Why do some portfolio companies pay sponsor consulting fees?", "a": "<p>Sponsor consulting (monitoring) fees are annual fees the portfolio company pays its PE owner. They’re common at firms with in-house consultants, operating partner teams or a dedicated consulting arm, and are set out in the investment agreement as payment for those advisory services.</p>", "p": 103}, {"id": "lbo-u1-49", "q": "What is the impact of the 2017 tax reform on the private equity industry?", "a": "<ul><li><b>Lower corporate tax rate:</b> cut from 35% to 21% (with smaller, less clear-cut changes for S corps and LLCs).</li><li><b>Interest deduction cap:</b> roughly, interest is deductible only up to about 30% of EBITDA — which offsets some of the benefit of lower rates for highly levered companies.</li><li><b>Faster depreciation:</b> more accelerated depreciation for tax, cutting upfront tax bills — especially helpful for capital-intensive businesses.</li><li><b>NOLs:</b> no more carrybacks, but carryforwards are now indefinite (previously 20 years); however, NOLs can only offset 80% of taxable income in a year (previously 100%).</li></ul>", "p": 103}, {"id": "lbo-u1-50", "q": "For private equity funds, the limited partnership is called a “blind pool.” What does this mean?", "a": "<p>In a blind pool, LPs commit capital without any say in which investments the fund makes — those decisions are left entirely to the GPs and the investment team. Once committed, LPs don’t review individual deals. That’s why LPs typically only invest with GPs they know, whose strategy, track record and past returns they understand.</p>", "p": 103}, {"id": "lbo-u1-51", "q": "In private equity, what is a capital call?", "a": "<p>A capital call (or drawdown) is the GP’s request for LPs to send the money they’ve committed, usually when a deal is about to close. LPs typically have a set window — around 7–12 days on average — to fund it.</p><p>Funds don’t hold all committed capital upfront; LPs keep it until called, often in low-risk, liquid investments in the meantime. An LP that fails to fund on time faces penalties, or may have to pay its remaining commitment immediately.</p>", "p": 103}]}], "lbo-u2": [{"id": "lbo-u2-l1", "title": "Building the LBO model", "pages": "104–105", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u2-1", "q": "Walk me through the mechanics of building an LBO model.", "a": "<p>An LBO model looks at a buyout funded with sponsor equity and new debt. It values the company’s equity pre-LBO, calculates returns to the new debt and equity providers, and shows the effect on the financial statements and credit ratios.</p><ol><li><b>Entry valuation:</b> entry multiple × LTM EBITDA (or, for a public target, the offer price per share).</li><li><b>Uses of funds:</b> payment to existing shareholders, refinancing pre-LBO debt, and transaction and financing fees.</li><li><b>Sources of funds:</b> how much debt is raised, with sponsor equity funding the rest.</li><li><b>Forecast</b> operations over the 5–7 year hold in a full three-statement model, so the new debt flows correctly through the income statement and cash flow statement.</li><li><b>Debt schedule:</b> tracks balances, paydowns and drawdowns based on excess cash or shortfalls each period.</li><li><b>Exit assumptions</b> — above all the exit EV/EBITDA multiple — combined with the balance sheet at exit give the sponsor’s <b>IRR</b> and <b>multiple of money (MoM)</b>.</li><li><b>Scenarios and sensitivities.</b> A common one backs into the maximum pre-LBO equity value the sponsor could pay given its hurdle rate and operating assumptions.</li></ol>", "p": 104}, {"id": "lbo-u2-2", "q": "What is the purpose of the “Sources & Uses” section of an LBO model?", "a": "<p>It shows how much capital the deal needs and how it will be funded. The two sides must balance.</p><p><b>Uses</b> — what’s being paid for: mainly buying the equity from existing shareholders, plus advisory (transaction) fees, financing fees and refinancing any existing debt.</p><p><b>Sources</b> — where the money comes from: the various debt tranches, the sponsor’s equity, excess cash on the target’s balance sheet, and sometimes management rollover.</p>", "p": 104}, {"id": "lbo-u2-3", "q": "How would you measure the credit health of a pre-LBO target company?", "a": "<p>With <b>leverage</b> and <b>interest coverage</b> ratios. Acceptable levels depend on the industry and lending environment, but typical LBO parameters are total debt/EBITDA of about <b>5.0–7.0x</b> and senior debt/EBITDA around <b>3.0x</b>.</p><p>For coverage, higher is better — interest coverage should be at least about <b>2.0x</b> in the first year after the buyout. From the sponsor’s side, these limits determine how much debt can be raised and therefore how much equity it has to put in.</p>", "p": 104}, {"id": "lbo-u2-4", "q": "Why is LBO analysis used as a floor valuation when analyzing company value using several valuation methodologies?", "a": "<p>Sponsors need to hit a <b>hurdle rate</b> — typically a minimum IRR of 15–25% — because of the leverage risk, the short holding period and their LPs’ return expectations. That required return is higher than the business’s ordinary cost of equity.</p><p>An LBO model tells you the maximum price a sponsor can pay and still hit that hurdle. Since the required return is higher, the implied value is <b>lower</b> than a DCF or comps would give — so it acts as a floor on valuation.</p>", "p": 105}, {"id": "lbo-u2-5", "q": "When analyzing the viability of undertaking an LBO, how do private equity firms estimate the company's value in the exit year?", "a": "<p>The usual assumption is that the sponsor exits at the <b>same EV/EBITDA multiple</b> it paid. If it buys at 10.0x, the exit 5–7 years later is assumed at 10.0x.</p><p>Because this assumption drives returns so heavily, it’s sensitised and IRRs are shown across a range of exit multiples.</p>", "p": 105}, {"id": "lbo-u2-6", "q": "If you had to choose two variables to sensitize in an LBO model, which ones would you pick?", "a": "<p>The <b>entry multiple</b> and the <b>exit multiple</b> — nothing else moves LBO returns as much. The ideal is to buy at a low multiple and sell at a higher one.</p><p>Other useful sensitivities: revenue growth, total leverage at entry and EBITDA margin.</p>", "p": 105}, {"id": "lbo-u2-7", "q": "What are the capex and net working capital requirement considerations for a private equity firm looking at a potential investment?", "a": "<p>High capex and high net working capital needs are both negatives for an LBO investor. Both consume cash, leaving less free cash flow to pay interest, repay debt and reinvest.</p><p>Traditional buyout targets should have limited capex, mostly maintenance. If a mature company still has high capex and working capital needs, it suggests an unfavourable cost structure — high maintenance spending and lots of cash tied up in operations.</p>", "p": 105}, {"id": "lbo-u2-8", "q": "If management decides to rollover equity, how would you calculate their new ownership stake and proceeds received at exit?", "a": "<p>The rollover is either a dollar input or a percentage of the new equity:</p><div class=\"formula\">Rollover equity = Total equity × Rollover %</div><div class=\"formula\">Management ownership = Rollover equity ÷ (Rollover equity + New sponsor equity)</div><div class=\"formula\">Management exit proceeds = Exit equity value × Management ownership %</div><p>Alternatively, proceeds can be structured as a share of the value created above the initial equity, or with a liquidation preference so management receives nothing until a return threshold is met.</p>", "p": 105}]}, {"id": "lbo-u2-l2", "title": "Returns: IRR, MoM & holding periods", "pages": "106–107", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u2-9", "q": "What are the two most common return metrics used by private equity firms?", "a": "<p><b>1. IRR (internal rate of return):</b> the effective compounded annual return on the investment. It’s time-weighted — it accounts for <i>when</i> proceeds are received.</p><div class=\"formula\">IRR = (FV ÷ PV)<sup>1/t</sup> − 1</div><p><b>2. Multiple of money (MoM)</b> — also called cash-on-cash return or MOIC: total cash received divided by total cash invested. It compares what the sponsor takes out with its initial equity.</p><div class=\"formula\">MoM = Total cash inflows ÷ Total cash outflows</div>", "p": 106}, {"id": "lbo-u2-10", "q": "If you're given the multiple of money (MoM) of an investment and the number of years the investment was held, what is the formula to calculate the internal rate of return (IRR)?", "a": "<div class=\"formula\">IRR = MoM<sup>1/t</sup> − 1</div><p>where t is the number of years held (assuming a single investment at the start and a single exit at the end). E.g. 2.0x over 5 years: 2<sup>1/5</sup> − 1 ≈ 15%.</p>", "p": 106}, {"id": "lbo-u2-11", "q": "What levers have a positive/negative impact on the IRR of an investment?", "a": "<table><tr><th>Raises IRR</th><th>Lowers IRR</th></tr><tr><td><b>Getting cash back earlier</b> — a dividend recap, an earlier-than-expected sale, cash (not PIK) interest for debt investors, annual monitoring fees to the sponsor.</td><td><b>Getting cash back later</b> — a delayed sale due to weak buyer interest or poor markets, PIK interest for debt investors.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Higher free cash flow</b> — strong revenue, EBITDA growth and better margins, so more debt is repaid during the hold.</td><td><b>Lower free cash flow</b> — missing forecasts or margin pressure (e.g. higher unit costs), so less debt is repaid.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Multiple expansion</b> — exiting above the entry multiple.</td><td><b>Multiple contraction</b> — exiting below the entry multiple.</td></tr></table>", "p": 106}, {"id": "lbo-u2-12", "q": "When measuring returns, why is it necessary to look at both the IRR and MoM?", "a": "<p>Each misses something:</p><ul><li><b>MoM ignores time.</b> A 3.0x return is great over five years, but it’s still “3.0x” if it took thirty.</li><li><b>IRR is very sensitive to timing.</b> A sponsor paying itself a dividend soon after the deal boosts IRR even if the overall MoM is mediocre — so IRR alone can mislead.</li></ul>", "p": 107}, {"id": "lbo-u2-13", "q": "Tell me how you would calculate IRR in Excel.", "a": "<p>You need the size of every cash inflow and outflow and the date of each.</p><p>Don’t say =IRR — it assumes every cash flow is exactly one year apart, which is unrealistic. Use <b>=XIRR(cash flows, dates)</b>, selecting the cash flow range and the matching dates.</p><p>The initial equity contribution is entered as a <b>negative</b> (an outflow); inflows — dividends, consulting fees and exit proceeds — are positive.</p>", "p": 107}, {"id": "lbo-u2-14", "q": "What is the difference between gross IRR and net IRR in private equity?", "a": "<p><b>Gross IRR:</b> returns at the fund level <i>before</i> management fees and carried interest — a pure measure of how well the GPs picked and managed investments. Often used to compare firms’ investment performance.</p><p><b>Net IRR:</b> returns to the LPs <i>after</i> fees and carry — what investors actually receive, on a time-weighted basis, so it matters more to LPs.</p>", "p": 107}, {"id": "lbo-u2-15", "q": "Tell me about the J-curve in private equity returns.", "a": "<p>The J-curve describes the shape of a fund’s net realised IRR over its life. Early on, it slopes sharply <b>downwards</b> — capital is going out into investments and management fees are being paid, with nothing coming back. As the fund starts exiting portfolio companies after their holding periods, the curve turns and rises — forming a “J”.</p>", "p": 107}, {"id": "lbo-u2-16", "q": "If a business that underwent an LBO has been operating as intended, why does the private equity firm not hold on to the investment for a longer duration (e.g., 10+ years)?", "a": "<p>PE funds have a finite life. The firm raises money in fund structures and has to return capital to LPs — usually several times — before raising the next fund. Unlike, say, pension funds targeting ~8–10% returns over long horizons, PE firms typically hold for 5–7 years.</p><p>IRR is also one of the main ways LPs judge PE firms, and holding too long drags IRR down, which hurts the firm’s marketing for future funds. Over short periods MoM matters more; over long periods a high IRR matters more.</p>", "p": 107}]}, {"id": "lbo-u2-l3", "title": "Exit timing, cash flow yield & LBO mechanics", "pages": "108–109", "cards": [{"id": "lbo-u2-17", "q": "In the situation when a private equity firm has the option to exit within a 1 to 2-year time frame, why might the firm be reluctant to proceed with the sale?", "a": "<p>Unless the returns are exceptional, a very quick exit means the capital has to be <b>redeployed</b> — and finding another good deal can take a long time, depending on the market. The firm has to weigh that opportunity cost, plus the transaction costs of another deal.</p>", "p": 108}, {"id": "lbo-u2-18", "q": "How would you calculate the levered free cash flow yield for private equity investment, and when would it be used?", "a": "<p>Less common than IRR and MoM, but useful for tracking how an investment is performing. From the sponsor’s view, levered FCF deducts interest and mandatory debt repayment:</p><div class=\"formula\">Levered FCF = EBITDA − Taxes − Interest − Capex − Increase in NWC − Mandatory debt amortisation</div><div class=\"formula\">Levered FCF yield = Levered FCF ÷ Initial equity investment</div><p>There’s no standard target — it varies with the industry, financing mix, ownership stake and required amortisation. Higher is better: it means cash is available to reinvest or pay a special dividend. The <b>year-over-year growth</b> matters more than the level, and levered FCF growing relative to the initial equity cheque is a sign of downside protection.</p>", "p": 108}, {"id": "lbo-u2-19", "q": "If we had not deducted interest and the mandatory debt amortization in calculating the free cash flow above, what metric would we be measuring?", "a": "<p><b>Unlevered free cash flow.</b> To get a matching yield, divide by enterprise value instead of equity:</p><div class=\"formula\">Unlevered FCF yield = Unlevered FCF ÷ Entry enterprise value</div><p>It shows how the business is performing operationally — how much cash is available to reinvest, pay down debt or pay dividends to equity holders.</p>", "p": 108}, {"id": "lbo-u2-20", "q": "How does the accounting treatment of financing fees differ from transaction fees in an LBO?", "a": "<p><b>Financing fees</b> (for raising debt or equity) are <b>capitalised and amortised</b> over the life of the debt, typically ~5–7 years.</p><p><b>Transaction fees</b> (M&amp;A advisory fees to banks or brokers, legal fees) can’t be amortised — they’re one-time expenses that reduce retained earnings.</p>", "p": 108}, {"id": "lbo-u2-21", "q": "If an acquirer writes-up the value of the intangible assets of a target, how are goodwill and amortization impacted?", "a": "<p>Goodwill is the plug between the purchase price and the fair value of the net assets acquired. If intangibles (patents, copyrights, trademarks) are written up, the identifiable assets are worth more, so <b>less goodwill</b> is created at closing.</p><p>The written-up intangibles are amortised, so <b>amortisation goes up</b>. Goodwill itself isn’t amortised by public companies under US GAAP (private companies can elect to, and it can be amortised for tax).</p>", "p": 109}, {"id": "lbo-u2-22", "q": "What does a cash sweep refer to in LBO modeling?", "a": "<p>Using excess free cash flow — after any revolver repayment — to make <b>optional</b> early repayments on debt, where the tranche allows it. Early repayment often carries a prepayment penalty, since the lender loses future interest.</p>", "p": 109}, {"id": "lbo-u2-23", "q": "What is the purpose of the minimum cash balance in an LBO model?", "a": "<p>Companies need some cash on hand to run day-to-day operations and meet working capital needs. The debt schedule’s formulas make sure cash never falls below this minimum. Because that cash has to stay on the balance sheet, it can’t help fund the deal — so it increases the funding required.</p><p><i>Interview note:</i> PE interviews almost always include an LBO model — anything from a one-hour test to a five-hour case study presented to the investment team — so practise every level, from a paper LBO up to a full integrated model.</p>", "p": 109}]}]}};
window.IBZY_MCQS_EXTRA = (window.IBZY_MCQS_EXTRA||[]).concat([{"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "In an LBO, most of the purchase price is typically funded with:", "o": ["The sponsor's equity", "Debt", "The target's cash", "Government grants"], "a": 1, "e": "A large debt package plus a smaller equity contribution from the sponsor."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "A typical PE holding period is about:", "o": ["1 year", "5–7 years", "15–20 years", "Indefinite"], "a": 1, "e": "Sponsors usually aim to exit within roughly 5–7 years."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "PE firms typically target returns (IRR) of roughly:", "o": ["5–8%", "20–25%", "50%+", "2–3%"], "a": 1, "e": "About 20–25% is a common hurdle."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Why does using more debt boost equity returns in an LBO?", "o": ["Debt is more expensive", "Debt is cheaper than equity and its paydown increases the equity's share of value", "Debt removes taxes entirely", "Lenders share the upside"], "a": 1, "e": "Lower-cost, tax-deductible debt means a smaller equity cheque that grows as debt is repaid."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "The typical LBO debt/equity mix in recent years is roughly:", "o": ["90/10", "60/40", "20/80", "50/50 always"], "a": 1, "e": "It has shifted from ~80/20 in the 1980s to ~60/40."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Which is NOT one of the three main LBO return levers?", "o": ["Debt paydown", "EBITDA growth", "Multiple expansion", "Increasing the share count"], "a": 3, "e": "Returns come from deleveraging, EBITDA growth and multiple expansion."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Which business is the best LBO candidate?", "o": ["Cyclical, capex-heavy, unpredictable cash flows", "Recurring revenue, strong FCF, low capex, defensible moat", "Pre-revenue start-up", "Company with one customer"], "a": 1, "e": "Predictable cash flow is needed to service heavy debt."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Why do PE firms like mature, low-growth industries?", "o": ["They grow fastest", "Strategics there buy to grow inorganically, providing exit options, and fragmentation suits buy-and-build", "They have no competition", "They need no management"], "a": 1, "e": "Stable cash flows plus acquisitive strategic buyers create viable exits."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l1", "d": "F", "q": "'Dry powder' refers to:", "o": ["Debt repaid", "Uncommitted capital a PE firm has available to invest", "Inventory", "A type of bond"], "a": 1, "e": "Using debt in deals preserves more capital for other investments."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "Typical LBO total leverage has been in the range of about:", "o": ["1–2x EBITDA", "5–7x EBITDA", "15–20x EBITDA", "0x"], "a": 1, "e": "Roughly 5.0–7.0x total debt/EBITDA, varying by industry."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Which is a reason a sponsor might use LESS leverage than available?", "o": ["To lower returns on purpose", "To leave capacity for a future dividend recap and limit default risk", "Lenders require it", "Equity is cheaper than debt"], "a": 1, "e": "Default risk, reputation, fund returns and future recaps all argue for some restraint."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Debt capacity analysis focuses mostly on:", "o": ["Best-case scenarios", "Downside scenarios and the stability of cash flows", "The sponsor's fees", "The company's share price"], "a": 1, "e": "Lenders size debt so obligations can be met even if EBITDA falls."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "F", "q": "The LBO 'tax shield' arises because:", "o": ["Dividends are tax-free", "Interest expense is tax-deductible", "PE firms pay no tax", "Debt reduces revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "More interest lowers taxable income."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Senior lenders typically lend up to about:", "o": ["2.0–3.0x EBITDA", "10x EBITDA", "0.5x EBITDA", "Unlimited"], "a": 0, "e": "Beyond that, costlier junior debt fills the gap."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Which exit usually fetches the highest valuation?", "o": ["Secondary buyout", "Sale to a strategic buyer", "Liquidation", "Dividend recap"], "a": 1, "e": "Strategics can pay for synergies."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Why is an IPO not an immediate exit?", "o": ["IPOs are illegal for PE", "Lock-up periods (90–180 days) and a long process mean the sponsor sells down over time", "IPO proceeds go to lenders", "IPOs always fail"], "a": 1, "e": "It's a path to an exit rather than an instant one."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Secondary buyouts tend to generate lower returns because:", "o": ["They use no debt", "The previous sponsor already captured many of the improvements", "They're always overpriced", "They can't be exited"], "a": 1, "e": "Less low-hanging fruit remains for the new owner."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l2", "d": "A", "q": "A dividend recap involves:", "o": ["Issuing equity to pay debt", "Raising new debt to pay the sponsor a dividend", "Selling the company", "Cancelling dividends"], "a": 1, "e": "It returns capital early, de-risking the investment and boosting IRR."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "Compared with an unlevered company, a highly levered company must be:", "o": ["Less disciplined on costs", "More disciplined on costs and conservative on capex", "Free to take more risks", "Indifferent to cash flow"], "a": 1, "e": "Fixed debt payments leave little margin for error."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "The standard conservative LBO assumption for the exit multiple is:", "o": ["Double the entry multiple", "Equal to the entry multiple", "Zero", "The industry's highest multiple"], "a": 1, "e": "Relying on multiple expansion is risky and outside the sponsor's control."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Why might a very large LBO see slight multiple contraction at exit?", "o": ["Larger companies are always worse", "Fewer buyers can afford it, reducing competition", "Debt increases at exit", "Taxes rise"], "a": 1, "e": "A smaller pool of capable buyers usually means a lower price."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "As a rule of thumb, no single customer should exceed about what share of revenue?", "o": ["50%", "5–10%", "25–30%", "1%"], "a": 1, "e": "Larger concentrations create too much risk if that customer is lost."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Why do many PE firms avoid companies previously owned by another sponsor?", "o": ["They're always bankrupt", "The easy improvements have often already been made, lowering returns", "Regulations forbid it", "They have no debt"], "a": 1, "e": "Secondary buyouts tend to return less."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "All else equal, which revenue is higher quality for an LBO?", "o": ["B2C monthly subscriptions", "B2B long-term contracts", "One-off consumer purchases", "Seasonal retail sales"], "a": 1, "e": "Businesses sign longer contracts, spend more and have higher switching costs."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Buying a company when sector valuations are temporarily inflated is risky mainly because:", "o": ["Debt is unavailable", "It makes exiting at a higher multiple much harder", "Taxes are higher", "Management leaves"], "a": 1, "e": "A high entry multiple undermines a key return lever."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l3", "d": "F", "q": "A key diligence question for an LBO target is:", "o": ["What is the CEO's favourite colour?", "What makes revenue recurring and is there a viable exit?", "How many offices does the bank have?", "What is the risk-free rate?"], "a": 1, "e": "Revenue quality, value-creation levers and exit options are central."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "In a management buyout, a large share of the new equity comes from:", "o": ["Public shareholders", "The existing management team", "Lenders", "The government"], "a": 1, "e": "Management believes it can run the business better under its own control."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Rollover equity is a positive sign because:", "o": ["It increases debt", "Management has skin in the game, aligning incentives", "It removes the need for diligence", "It guarantees returns"], "a": 1, "e": "Management reinvesting signals confidence in the upside."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "Why might a PE firm favour term loans over subordinated notes?", "o": ["Higher interest rates", "Lower cost, prepayment flexibility and fewer restrictive incurrence covenants", "They're unsecured", "They're equity"], "a": 1, "e": "Cheaper, flexible senior debt is maximised before junior debt."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "Buyout firms generally prefer:", "o": ["Rapid but volatile growth", "Stable, predictable revenue", "Pre-revenue companies", "Highly cyclical revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Heavy leverage requires dependable cash flow."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "An advantage of capital-intensive businesses for PE is that:", "o": ["They need little capital", "High barriers to entry and more collateral for borrowing", "They have no competition by law", "Capex is tax-free"], "a": 1, "e": "PP&E deters entrants and expands the borrowing base."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "'Multiple arbitrage' in a roll-up means:", "o": ["Selling at a lower multiple", "Buying add-ons at lower multiples than the platform trades at, so their cash flows are revalued upward", "Using no debt", "Issuing more shares"], "a": 1, "e": "Smaller targets are cheaper; combined with the platform, they're valued at its higher multiple."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "An investment triples in 5 years. The IRR is roughly:", "o": ["15%", "25%", "45%", "60%"], "a": 1, "e": "3.0x over 5 years ≈ 24.6%."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "C", "q": "An investment doubles in 3 years. The IRR is roughly:", "o": ["15%", "25%", "35%", "45%"], "a": 1, "e": "2.0x over 3 years ≈ 26%, so ~25%."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l4", "d": "F", "q": "Using the Rule of 72, money doubles at 9% in about:", "o": ["6 years", "8 years", "12 years", "9 years"], "a": 1, "e": "72 ÷ 9 = 8."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "F", "q": "Which is a typical LP in a private equity fund?", "o": ["A retail day trader", "A pension fund", "The portfolio company", "A credit rating agency"], "a": 1, "e": "LPs include pensions, endowments, insurers, sovereign wealth funds and HNWIs."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "C", "q": "Record-high dry powder in PE usually leads to:", "o": ["Lower purchase multiples", "Higher purchase multiples as competition for deals increases", "No change in prices", "More bankruptcies immediately"], "a": 1, "e": "Lots of capital chasing few deals pushes prices up."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "C", "q": "A key downside of proprietary deal sourcing is:", "o": ["Higher prices from auctions", "It's labour-intensive and owners of good companies often don't want to sell", "It's illegal", "It uses no relationships"], "a": 1, "e": "It takes lots of outreach to convince owners to sell."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "F", "q": "In '2 and 20', the 20% refers to:", "o": ["The annual management fee", "Carried interest on profits above a hurdle", "The tax rate", "Leverage"], "a": 1, "e": "GPs get 20% of profits; the 2% is the management fee."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "C", "q": "In a classic waterfall, what is paid out FIRST?", "o": ["GP carried interest", "LPs' capital plus the hurdle (preferred) return", "Management bonuses", "The catch-up"], "a": 1, "e": "LPs are made whole and receive the hurdle before GPs earn carry."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "A", "q": "A clawback provision allows:", "o": ["GPs to reclaim fees from LPs", "LPs to recover carried interest GPs were overpaid", "Lenders to seize assets", "Management to buy back shares"], "a": 1, "e": "If later deals underperform, excess carry is returned."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "C", "q": "How does a recapitalisation differ from an LBO in accounting?", "o": ["Recaps create more goodwill", "Recaps aren't acquisitions: no asset write-ups or goodwill, often leaving negative equity", "They're identical", "Recaps can't use debt"], "a": 1, "e": "The asset base carries over unchanged."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "C", "q": "After the 2017 tax reform, interest deductibility is limited to roughly:", "o": ["10% of revenue", "30% of EBITDA", "100% of interest", "Zero"], "a": 1, "e": "This partly offsets the lower corporate rate for levered companies."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · Private Equity Investing", "l": "lbo-u1-l5", "d": "F", "q": "A capital call is:", "o": ["A fund's request for LPs to send committed capital", "A call option on stock", "A lender demanding repayment", "A sponsor consulting fee"], "a": 0, "e": "Committed capital is drawn down when deals close."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "The largest 'use' of funds in most LBOs is:", "o": ["Financing fees", "Buying out the existing equity", "Capex", "Dividends"], "a": 1, "e": "Purchasing the target's equity is the main use."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "Which is a 'source' of funds in an LBO?", "o": ["Transaction fees", "Refinancing existing debt", "Sponsor equity", "Purchase of equity"], "a": 2, "e": "Sources include debt, sponsor equity, excess cash and rollover."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "A typical minimum interest coverage ratio in year 1 of an LBO is around:", "o": ["0.5x", "2.0x", "10x", "20x"], "a": 1, "e": "Coverage of at least ~2.0x is a common rule of thumb."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "LBO analysis is considered a 'floor' valuation because:", "o": ["It ignores debt", "Sponsors' high required returns imply a lower price than DCF or comps", "It uses book value", "It always gives the highest value"], "a": 1, "e": "Higher hurdle rates mean lower present values."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "F", "q": "The default assumption for the exit multiple in an LBO is:", "o": ["Double the entry", "Equal to the entry multiple", "Half the entry", "The highest peer multiple"], "a": 1, "e": "Exit = entry is the conservative base case, then sensitised."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Which two variables have the biggest effect on LBO returns?", "o": ["Tax rate and capex", "Entry and exit multiples", "Number of shares and dividends", "Interest rate and NWC"], "a": 1, "e": "Buying cheap and selling dear drives returns most."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "High capex and working capital needs are negative for an LBO because:", "o": ["They raise EBITDA", "They consume cash that could pay down debt", "They're tax-deductible", "They reduce leverage"], "a": 1, "e": "Less free cash flow means slower deleveraging."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l1", "d": "C", "q": "Management rolls over $20m and the sponsor invests $80m. Management's ownership is:", "o": ["20%", "25%", "80%", "16%"], "a": 0, "e": "20 ÷ (20 + 80) = 20%."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "MoM (multiple of money) is calculated as:", "o": ["Total cash inflows ÷ total cash outflows", "IRR × years", "EBITDA ÷ debt", "Exit multiple ÷ entry multiple"], "a": 0, "e": "It's the cash-on-cash return."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "An investment returns 2.0x over 5 years. The IRR is roughly:", "o": ["10%", "15%", "20%", "40%"], "a": 1, "e": "2^(1/5) − 1 ≈ 14.9%."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Which action increases IRR but not necessarily MoM?", "o": ["A dividend recap shortly after the deal", "Multiple contraction", "A delayed exit", "Lower EBITDA"], "a": 0, "e": "Earlier cash returns boost IRR via timing."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Why isn't MoM sufficient on its own?", "o": ["It ignores the time value of money", "It's always negative", "It ignores exit proceeds", "It's too complex"], "a": 0, "e": "The same multiple over 5 vs. 30 years is very different."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "F", "q": "In Excel, the better function for IRR with irregular dates is:", "o": ["=IRR", "=XIRR", "=NPV", "=PMT"], "a": 1, "e": "XIRR uses actual dates; IRR assumes annual intervals."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "Net IRR differs from gross IRR because it:", "o": ["Includes leverage", "Is after management fees and carried interest", "Is before fees", "Ignores timing"], "a": 1, "e": "Net IRR is what LPs actually receive."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "C", "q": "The early downward slope of the PE J-curve is caused by:", "o": ["Exits", "Capital deployed and management fees with no returns yet", "Dividend recaps", "Rising valuations"], "a": 1, "e": "Outflows come first; exits come later."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l2", "d": "A", "q": "Why don't PE firms usually hold companies for 10+ years?", "o": ["It's illegal", "Funds must return capital to LPs, and long holds drag down IRR", "Debt can't last that long", "Taxes rise after 5 years"], "a": 1, "e": "Fund structures and IRR-based performance push for 5–7 year exits."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "A PE firm might hesitate to exit after just 1–2 years because:", "o": ["It's prohibited", "It must find a new deal to redeploy capital, which takes time and costs money", "Returns are always negative", "Lenders forbid it"], "a": 1, "e": "Opportunity cost and transaction costs weigh on quick flips."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Levered FCF yield in PE is calculated as:", "o": ["Levered FCF ÷ initial equity investment", "Unlevered FCF ÷ EV", "EBITDA ÷ debt", "Net income ÷ revenue"], "a": 0, "e": "It compares cash available to equity with the equity invested."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Unlevered FCF yield uses which denominator?", "o": ["Initial equity", "Entry enterprise value", "Total debt", "Revenue"], "a": 1, "e": "Unlevered cash flows match enterprise value."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "F", "q": "In an LBO, financing fees are:", "o": ["Expensed immediately", "Capitalised and amortised over the debt's life", "Added to goodwill", "Ignored"], "a": 1, "e": "Transaction fees, by contrast, are expensed."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "Writing up a target's intangible assets will:", "o": ["Increase goodwill", "Decrease goodwill and increase amortisation", "Have no effect", "Decrease amortisation"], "a": 1, "e": "Goodwill is the plug; higher identifiable assets mean less goodwill."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "F", "q": "A cash sweep uses excess cash to:", "o": ["Pay dividends", "Make optional early debt repayments", "Buy back shares", "Fund capex"], "a": 1, "e": "It accelerates deleveraging."}, {"t": "LBO", "u": "LBO · LBO Modeling", "l": "lbo-u2-l3", "d": "C", "q": "A minimum cash balance in an LBO:", "o": ["Reduces the funding needed", "Increases the funding needed, since that cash can't fund the deal", "Has no effect", "Is paid to the sponsor"], "a": 1, "e": "Cash kept for operations is not available as a source."}]);
